   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>As its name implies, <code>SysAllocString</code> allocates its memory, it does not \"adopt\" its argument's memory. BSTRs are size-prefixed <em>and</em> null-terminated, so \"adopting\" a c-style string is impossible, as there is no space for the size prefix.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2677136,
   "parent_id": 2677097,
   "author_user_id": 241536,
   "author_display_name": "John Dibling",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2677136",
   "score": 3,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-20T17:09:41Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-04-20T17:09:41Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p>Yes, <code>delete</code> the memory.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2677183,
   "parent_id": 2677097,
   "author_user_id": 74815,
   "author_display_name": "i_am_jorf",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2677183",
   "score": 14,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-20T17:17:16Z",
   "last_edit_utc": "2024-02-07T16:19:11Z",
   "last_activity_utc": "2024-02-07T16:19:11Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
   "body_html": "<p>SysAllocString(), from the <a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms891285.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">documentation</a>, behaves like this:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This function allocates a new string\nand copies the passed string into it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>So, yes, once you've called SysAllocString you can free your original character array, as the data has been copied into the newly allocated BSTR.</p>\n<p>The proper way to free a string of <code>wchar_t</code> allocated with <code>new[]</code> is to use <code>delete[]</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>wchar_t *hs = new wchar_t[20];\n...\ndelete[] hs;\n</code></pre>\n<p>The proper way to free a <code>BSTR</code> is with <a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms891288.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">SysFreeString()</a>:</p>\n<pre><code>BSTR bs = SysAllocString(hs);\n...\nSysFreeString(bs);\n</code></pre>\n<p>While you're new to BSTRs, you should read <a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/archive/blogs/ericlippert/erics-complete-guide-to-bstr-semantics\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Eric's Complete Guide to BSTR Semantics</a>.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 2677186,
   "parent_id": 2677097,
   "author_user_id": 12711,
   "author_display_name": "Michael Burr",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/2677186",
   "score": 4,
   "created_utc": "2010-04-20T17:17:45Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2010-04-20T17:17:45Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 2.5",
   "body_html": "<p><a href=\"http://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/ms891285.aspx\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">The docs for <code>SysAllocString()</code></a> are pretty clear:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n  <p>This function allocates a new string and copies the passed string into it. </p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>The string data you pass in is copied - <code>SysAllocString()</code> doesn't use it after it's completed - you're free to deallocate or modify that buffer.</p>\n"
  },
